From: Neanderfunk (Backport)
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: mediatek-ge: disable broken EEE on the MT7530 PHY

Backport for Linux 5.15 (OpenWrt 23.05 / Gluon 2023.2) of the approach of
OpenWrt PR #25058 "kernel: disable broken EEE on the MT7530 PHY"
(backport-6.18/786-v7.3-net-phy-mediatek-ge-disable-EEE-on-MT7530-PHY.patch,
netdev: 20260904202800.3410838-1-vladislav.karmanov.dev@gmail.com,
Reviewed-by: Andrew Lunn), which supersedes PR #22647.

The MT7530 internal GE PHY (also the switch of MT7621) has broken EEE: on a
2-pair cable where both ends advertise gigabit, the port loops instead of
falling back to 100 Mbps; some 100 Mbps partners never get a stable link.

Differences to the 6.18 patch, forced by 5.15:
- phy_disable_eee() does not exist; phydev->eee_broken_modes is set in
  config_init instead (phy_probe() resets it after ->probe), so
  genphy_config_aneg() keeps EEE off, also against ethtool.
- phylib on 5.15 does not renegotiate when only the EEE advertisement
  changes. mtk_gephy_config_init() has always cleared it, yet a link that
  came up with EEE kept it. So the advertisement is cleared in ->probe and
  in config_init, and autonegotiation is restarted if it was set.
- Errors are not fatal (the port must not be lost over this).

Refs: https://github.com/openwrt/openwrt/issues/16551
Refs: https://github.com/openwrt/openwrt/pull/22647
---
 drivers/net/phy/mediatek-ge.c | 42 +
 1 file changed, 42 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/net/phy/mediatek-ge.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/mediatek-ge.c
@@ -44,8 +44,49 @@
 	phy_write_mmd(phydev, MDIO_MMD_VEND1, 0xa6, 0x300);
 }
 
+/* The MT7530 internal GE PHY has broken EEE: with EEE advertised, some
+ * link partners fail to establish a stable link (on a 2-pair cable,
+ * 1000BASE-T training fails and the port loops instead of falling back to
+ * 100 Mbps). MediaTek recommends disabling EEE on this PHY.
+ *
+ * mtk_gephy_config_init() already clears the advertisement, but on this
+ * kernel phylib does not renegotiate when only the EEE advertisement
+ * changes, so a link that came up with EEE (e.g. from the PHY's reset
+ * default) kept it. Clear it and restart autonegotiation if it was set.
+ * Failures are not fatal: the port must not be lost over this.
+ */
+static void mt7530_phy_disable_eee(struct phy_device *phydev)
+{
+	int adv;
+
+	adv = phy_read_mmd(phydev, MDIO_MMD_AN, MDIO_AN_EEE_ADV);
+	if (adv <= 0)
+		return;
+
+	if (phy_write_mmd(phydev, MDIO_MMD_AN, MDIO_AN_EEE_ADV, 0))
+		return;
+
+	genphy_restart_aneg(phydev);
+}
+
+static int mt7530_phy_probe(struct phy_device *phydev)
+{
+	/* As early as possible, before anything negotiates EEE. */
+	mt7530_phy_disable_eee(phydev);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static int mt7530_phy_config_init(struct phy_device *phydev)
 {
+	/* phy_probe() resets eee_broken_modes after ->probe, so set it here:
+	 * genphy_config_aneg() then keeps EEE off, also against ethtool.
+	 * config_init also runs again after a PHY reset, which brings back the
+	 * EEE-advertising hardware default.
+	 */
+	phydev->eee_broken_modes = MDIO_EEE_100TX | MDIO_EEE_1000T;
+	mt7530_phy_disable_eee(phydev);
+
 	mtk_gephy_config_init(phydev);
 
 	/* Increase post_update_timer */
@@ -106,6 +147,7 @@
 	{
 		PHY_ID_MATCH_EXACT(0x03a29412),
 		.name		= "MediaTek MT7530 PHY",
+		.probe		= mt7530_phy_probe,
 		.config_init	= mt7530_phy_config_init,
 		/* Interrupts are handled by the switch, not the PHY
 		 * itself.
